# Methodology

This folder describes the method by which the knowledge base is turned from
prose into something that can be counted, compared and checked. The cards in
`knowledge/` say things. The method records what each card says, how sure it
is, where two cards part, and what several cards are saying in common.

It is the rudimentary version. Each step is done in the plainest way that
runs today, and each document says what its step cannot yet see.

## The steps

| Step | What is done | Operation | Described in |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Each concept card is cut into the claims it makes | decomposition | [claims](claims.hmd) |
| 2 | Each claim gets an evidence grade and a credence | evaluation | [claims](claims.hmd) |
| 3 | Claims that fit are also written as atoms | translation | [partial logic](partial-logic.hmd) |
| 4 | Every link is scored by how far its two cards disagree | evaluation | [cross scores](cross-scores.hmd) |
| 5 | What recurs across cards is stated once, as a north star | abstraction | [north stars](north-stars.hmd) |
| 6 | The stars are checked as a cover of the knowledge base | evaluation | [north stars](north-stars.hmd) |
| 7 | The concepts that carry the argument are drawn as one map | synthesis | [story map](story-map.hmd) |
| 8 | Headers and links are read as a typed graph and checked against a schema | deduction | the card `knowledge-graph` in the knowledge base |
| 9 | Atoms are derived from asserted atoms by rules, and each derivation is set against the denials | deduction | [derivation](derivation.hmd) |

The third column names the cognitive operation each step is, in the vault's
own vocabulary. The method uses the operations the vault defines, on the
vault. An agent could perform any of them, and so could a person. The method
does not depend on which, and it fixes what goes in and what comes out of each
step so that the result can be checked either way.

Step 5 is abstraction and not summarization or generalization. The difference
is the one the abstraction card draws, and [north stars](north-stars.hmd)
says how it is held to.

## Where things are

| Thing | Place |
|---|---|
| Claims, credences, atoms | the header of each concept card, under `metadata.claims` |
| North stars | cards in `knowledge/stars/` |
| The schema of the graph | the header of `knowledge/meta/knowledge-graph.hmd` |
| The rules of inference | the same header, under `metadata.rules` |
| The scripts: derivation, and distance between claim texts | `scripts/derive.py` and `scripts/claim_distance.py` |
| Types and functions shared by the queries | `.hql/lib/claims/mod.hql` and `.hql/lib/kg/mod.hql` |
| The queries | `.hql/queries/` |
| The proofs, in Lean | `scripts/lean/` |

## The queries

All of these run on the current query language.

| Query | Answers |
|---|---|
| [claims](../.hql/queries/claims.hql) | What does the vault claim? Every claim with its address. |
| [atom status](../.hql/queries/atom-status.hql) | Which atoms are asserted, denied, or both? |
| [cross scores](../.hql/queries/cross-scores.hql) | How far do the two cards of each link disagree? |
| [two-way links](../.hql/queries/two-way-links.hql) | Which pairs of cards declare a one-way relation in both directions? |
| [recurring terms](../.hql/queries/recurring-terms.hql) | Which predicates and things recur across cards? |
| [star cover](../.hql/queries/star-cover.hql) | Do the north stars cover the concepts, and does any star lie inside another? |
| [schema check](../.hql/queries/schema-check.hql) | Does every arrow of the graph have a signature the schema allows? |

One design waits for the language: `claim-contradictions.hql` in the future
queries compares claims by meaning, which reaches the claims that have no
atom.

## What the numbers say today

- 54 cards carry claims: 53 concepts and the card that describes the graph.
  The eight star cards each carry one more, the statement of the star.
- 370 claims on concept cards. 254 of them have an atom.
- 206 distinct atoms across all cards, of which one is asserted by one card and denied by
  another.
- 9 rules of inference derive 7 atoms that no claim states, and none of the
  7 is denied.
- 503 links join two cards that carry claims. 97 of them join cards that speak
  on a shared atom, and 16 of those show a disagreement above zero. The
  largest is 0.24.
- 13 pairs of cards declare a one-way relation in both directions.
- The typed graph has 454 nodes and 1,648 arrows, and every arrow conforms to
  the schema.

The low disagreement is partly real, since the cards were written from one
line of discussion, and partly the limit of the method: atoms are compared
only when they are the same string, and the credences are still the priors
their evidence grade gave them. The thirteen two-way links were found outside
the claims entirely. The inconsistency the vault has is more likely to be in
what the method cannot compare yet than in what it can.

## The logic under derivation

[From distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd) reads a distance with a
direction as an entailment: `a ⊑ b` when the distance from `a` to `b` is zero.
It gives the rules by which atoms are derived from other atoms, and each
rule is proved in Lean. [Derivation](derivation.hmd) is the step built on it:
the rules as data, a script that applies them, and what they find. The script
is Python, and no query derives anything yet.

## What comes next

- Atoms written with shared arguments, so that the rules of
  [derivation](derivation.hmd) have chains to follow. Today they yield seven
  atoms.
- More rules between predicates, each kept only if every atom it gives is
  read and none is false.
- Derivation as a query, when the query language can express a fixed point.
- Credences moved by hand, with a reason recorded, where a claim has been
  tested or challenged.
- Comparison of claims by meaning, for the third of the claims with no atom.
- Tighter signatures in the schema, each one a claim about the vault that the
  check then enforces.
- The check feeding a revision of the schema through a reviewed gate, which
  is the step that would make the graph's description of itself take part in
  changing it.
